  SUMMARY:  This evaluation is based on a theoretical analysis    8237 1451  165
that utilizes Hauser-Feshbach statistical theory, including       8237 1451  166
direct-reaction and preequilibrium processes, and that covers     8237 1451  167
the incident neutron energy range from 1 keV to 200 MeV [Vo94].   8237 1451  168
Coupled-channel optical model calculations are used to obtain     8237 1451  169
neutron transmission coefficients for the Hauser-Feshbach         8237 1451  170
calculations and for the elastic and inelastic neutron angular    8237 1451  171
distributions, and spherical optical potentials are used to       8237 1451  172
obtain transmission coefficients for charged-particles.  Some     8237 1451  173
data from ENDF/B-VI are retained, in particular, the neutron      8237 1451  174
total cross section below 5 MeV, the Reich-Moore resolved         8237 1451  175
resonance parameter evaluation below 1 MeV, and the covariance    8237 1451  176
data describing cross section uncertainties.                      8237 1451  177
   Cross sections and spectra for individual reactions are        8237 1451  178
included for reactions that exceed a cross section of             8237 1451  179
approximately 0.1 mb at any energy.  Multiplicities and emission  8237 1451  180
energy spectra are given for gamma rays, particles, and recoil    8237 1451  181
nuclei emitted in each reaction, utilizing File 6 of the ENDF/B-  8237 1451  182
6 format (Ro91).  Energy-angle-correlated spectra are given for   8237 1451  183
all outgoing particles.                                           8237 1451  184
                                                                  8237 1451  185
  OPTICAL MODEL CALCULATIONS:  For incident and exiting           8237 1451  186
neutrons, the coupled-channel code ECIS (Ra72) was used to        8237 1451  187
calculate transmission coefficients as well as differential       8237 1451  188
elastic and inelastic cross sections in the framework of a        8237 1451  189
vibrational model.  The optical potential described by Vonach et  8237 1451  190
al. [Vo94] and the deformation parameters given in Atomic and Nuc.8237 1451  191
Data Tables were utilized for neutron energies up to 30 MeV.      8237 1451  192
All collective excitations were assumed to be one-phonon states.  8237 1451  193
Direct reaction components were calculated for approximately      8237 1451  194
100 states of 208Pb using the ECIS95 code.                        8237 1451  195
  The SCAT2 code [Be92] using global spherical optical model      8237 1451  196
potentials was used to calculate transmission coefficients for    8237 1451  197
protons [Be69], deuterons [Pe63], tritons [Be71], and alpha       8237 1451  198
particles [Mc66].                                                 8237 1451  199
                                                                  8237 1451  200
  HAUSER-FESHBACH STATISTICAL/PREEQUILIBRIUM THEORY               8237 1451  201
CALCULATIONS:  The COMNUC code [Du70] was used to calculate       8237 1451  202
neutron angular distributions below 10 MeV for discrete states    8237 1451  203
and to calculate width-fluctuation correction factors for use in  8237 1451  204
the GNASH analysis.  The GNASH code (Yo92) was used for all       8237 1451  205
cross section calculations.  Preequilibrium corrections were      8237 1451  206
performed in the course of the GNASH calculations using the       8237 1451  207
exciton model of Kalbach (Ka77,Ka85).  Nominal parameters were    8237 1451  208
used for the exciton model calculation: a matrix element          8237 1451  209
normalization constant of 250 MeV**3 was used at 14 MeV, and      8237 1451  210
was phased into a value of about 188 MeV**3 at 30 MeV.            8237 1451  211
The nuclear state densities were set to A/13 (/MeV) in the        8237 1451  212
asymptotic limit where shell effects are washed out.  Compound    8237 1451  213
nucleus contributions to cross sections and angular               8237 1451  214
distributions were combined with the direct reaction and          8237 1451  215
preequilibrium components.                                        8237 1451  216
  Discrete level data from nuclear data sheets were matched to    8237 1451  217
continuum level densities using the formulation of Ignatyuk       8237 1451  218
(Ig75) and pairing and shell parameters from the Cook (Co67)      8237 1451  219
analysis.  The Cook pairing energy for 207Pb was adjusted         8237 1451  220
slightly to improve agreement with the neutron emission spectrum  8237 1451  221
evaluation of Pavlik and Vonach [Pa88].                           8237 1451  222
  Neutron and charged-particle transmission coefficients were     8237 1451  223
obtained from the optical potentials, as discussed above.         8237 1451  224
Gamma-ray transmission coefficients were calculated using the     8237 1451  225
Kopecky-Uhl model (Ko90).                                         8237 1451  226
  Calculations were performed for all significant reactions       8237 1451  227
producing neutrons, protons, deuterons, tritons, alpha            8237 1451  228
particles, and gamma rays for incident neutrons between 0.001     8237 1451  229
and 30 MeV.  At the highest energies, approximately 22 compound   8237 1451  230
nuclei had to be included, leading to ~43 reaction paths.  Semi-  8237 1451  231
direct processes were included in the radiative capture           8237 1451  232
calculations of cross section and photon-emission spectra above   8237 1451  233
a few MeV. Angle-integrated emission spectra for recoil nuclei    8237 1451  234
are given for all significant reactions.                          8237 1451  235
  The angular distribution systematics by Kalbach (Ka88) were     8237 1451  236
used to describe the angular distributions for all continuum      8237 1451  237
particles and for discrete particle reactions at higher energies  8237 1451  238
from non-vibrational states.                                      8237 1451  239
